5, 0.01290130615234375, -0.017578125, -0.005786895751953125, -0.0249176025390625, 0.0045013427734375, -0.02227783203125, -0.0035991668701171875, 0.05609130859375, 0.01210784912109375, 0.0052337646484375, 0.01062774658203125, 0.0210113525390625, 0.018707275390625, 0.024993896484375, -0.0274200439453125, -0.0177459716796875, 0.0161895751953125, 0.00910186767578125, 0.0160675048828125, 0.00013566017150878906, -0.004512786865234375, -0.01517486572265625, 0.0104827880859375, 0.005481719970703125, 0.00580596923828125, 0.003520965576171875, -4.172325134277344e-06, -0.0008683204650878906, 0.005550384521484375, -0.01058197021484375, -0.0019397735595703125, 0.0012998580932617188, 0.01068115234375, 0.0201263427734375, 0.008056640625, -0.01312255859375, 0.00894927978515625, 0.023345947265625, 0.00330352783203125, 0.053955078125, -0.040130615234375, 0.0185699462890625, 0.01389312744140625, -0.042388916015625, 0.01303863525390625, -0.0107269287109375, 0.0111083984375, 0.01509857177734375, -0.032318115234375, 0.00914764404296875, -0.0038738250732421875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "District heating networks are a central tool to achieve low-carbon heat supplies, but their efficient operation is challenged by increasingly heterogeneous, partially time-varying renewable sources, the integration of thermal storage, and the presence of meshed topologies. While previous work has addressed operational optimization for simpler or linearized models, there remains a gap in efficiently solving the global, multi-period nonlinear optimization of realistic, cyclic district heating networks with multiple suppliers and storage. This paper addresses this gap by proposing new algorithmic techniques for the global optimization of such networks over multiple time steps, based on a stationary nonlinear hydraulic-thermal network model that captures key physical constraints, including nonlinear pressure drops, temperature-dependent mixing, and realistic supply/return temperature bounds.\n\nTo accelerate the spatial branch-and-bound algorithm for a single time step, we introduce several methodological innovations: (1) exclusion of cyclic flow by adding constraints that prevent non-physical circulation, thereby reducing the feasible space; (2) exploitation of symmetry between supply and return networks by identifying and merging equivalent configurations, which avoids redundant search; (3) reduction of temperature mixing constraints by aggregating nodes with similar mixing behavior, thus simplifying the model; (4) novel primal heuristics that quickly generate high-quality feasible solutions to guide the search; and (5) tailored branching rules that prioritize variables with the greatest impact on feasibility and objective value. These techniques are designed to address the combinatorial and nonlinear complexity inherent in realistic district heating networks.\n\nWe evaluate our approach on both generated and real-world benchmark instances. The generated instances range from 10 to 50 nodes, include cycles, and feature up to 5 suppliers, reflecting the complexity of modern networks. On these, our methods increase the number of solved instances from 18/40 to 39/40 and reduce median runtime from 1200 seconds to 540 seconds. For real-world instances (with up to 30 nodes and 3 suppliers), our algorithm finds globally optimal solutions with optimality gaps below 0.1% in under 900 seconds, providing both solution quality guarantees and practical runtimes.\n\nFor multi-period operation with storage, we investigate a time decomposition approach that decouples the problem into sequential single-period subproblems linked by storage state variables. This approach is provably optimal under the practical assumption that storage charging/discharging rates are not saturated at the solution, and that network constraints do not induce inter-temporal coupling beyond storage. However, in cases where these assumptions are violated\u2014such as when storage limits are frequently active or when strong inter-temporal dependencies exist\u2014the approach may yield suboptimal solutions. On a small example network (10 nodes, 2 suppliers, 3 time steps), the decomposition computes optimal solutions in less than a second, whereas solving the full time-coupled problem exceeds one hour. This demonstrates the potential for significant computational savings, though further work is needed to assess scalability to larger networks.\n\nIn summary, this paper closes a key gap in the global optimization of realistic, cyclic district heating networks with storage by introducing and evaluating novel algorithmic enhancements. Our results demonstrate substantial improvements in both solution rate and runtime, with rigorous guarantees on solution quality for practical network sizes."}}